          <p>
            Two notes on the Gold Standard column. Optimum Nutrition's own page shows the supplement facts only as an image, but it confirms the 24 g of protein and 5.5 g of BCAAs per scoop. Some grocery listings still show an older panel with a 30.4 g scoop and 50 mg of sodium, so the tub in your hand may differ slightly depending on the lot. Neither brand publishes a leucine figure, so we cannot compare the two on that.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the Ghost side, Milk Chocolate was marked sold out on Ghost's store when our database was checked, while flavors such as Trix and Lucky Charms Cereal Milk were in stock at the same $54.99 for a 2 lb tub. Ghost labels Whey gluten-free and soy-free, though one retailer notes that some flavors are excluded from the gluten-free claim, so check the flavor you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has More Protein per Scoop?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ghost Whey has 1 g more protein per scoop, but Gold Standard has more protein per gram of powder. Per the labels, Ghost gives 25 g in a 35 g rounded scoop and Gold Standard gives 24 g in a 31 g scoop. That puts Ghost at about 71% protein by weight and Gold Standard at about 77%.
          </p>

          <p>
            The per-scoop number is the one printed largest on the tub, but it is the less useful of the two. Any brand can raise protein per serving by making the scoop bigger. Protein by weight tells you how much of what you pay for is actually protein, and the rest of each Ghost scoop is cocoa, flavoring, enzymes, gums and salt. That is why protein density is the largest single part of our <a href='/methodology/'>Label Score</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice the 1 g gap is easy to close. If you want exactly 25 g from Gold Standard, a scoop and a sliver more gets you there for about 125 calories, which is still under Ghost's 130. If you aim for 30 g after training, you need about 1.2 scoops of Ghost (roughly 156 calories) or 1.25 scoops of Gold Standard (about 150 calories). Either way the difference is a few calories, not a reason to choose one over the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both blends use the same three whey forms: isolate, concentrate and hydrolyzed whey. Neither brand discloses the ratio, but both list isolate first, which by label rules means it is the largest of the three. Expect very similar amino acid profiles and digestion speed from both. Optimum Nutrition states 5.5 g of BCAAs per scoop; Ghost does not publish a BCAA or leucine figure on the panel we read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ghost Protein Powder vs Optimum Nutrition: Which Is Cheaper per Gram of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition is cheaper per gram of protein, by about a third, as long as you buy a large tub. At $79.99 for 58 servings at Target, Gold Standard works out to about $1.44 per 25 g of protein. At $54.99 for 26 servings on Ghost's store, Ghost Whey costs about $2.12 per 25 g, roughly 47% more for the same protein. Both prices were recorded in our database on 1 November 2026.
          </p>

