          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard does value, density and testing well, and falls short on soy and small-tub pricing. It gives 24 g of protein at about 77% protein by weight, costs about $1.44 per 25 g in the 58-serving tub, has two NSF Certified for Sport flavors and offers about 24 flavors. That earns it a Label Score of 78 out of 100, among the stronger results for a mainstream whey blend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its weak points are smaller but real. It lists soy as an allergen because it may use soy lecithin. The certification covers only Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream on the NSF listing we checked, not every flavor. The small tub costs more per gram than Ghost, and labels vary by lot and packaging, so some tubs show a slightly different panel. Like Ghost, it uses an artificial sweetener and artificial flavor.
          </p>

