                <p>
                  Both should mix with a shaker bottle, according to buyer reviews and their labels. Each uses lecithin, which helps powder disperse in water. Ghost also adds cellulose and xanthan gums, which give a thicker shake that some buyers prefer. We have not run our own mixing test on either, so treat this as buyer feedback rather than a lab result.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Ghost Whey or Gold Standard have soy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Gold Standard lists soy as an allergen because it may use soy lecithin, per the label we read. Ghost uses sunflower lecithin and labels Ghost Whey soy-free. Both contain milk. If you avoid soy for allergy reasons, Ghost is the one of the two that fits, but always check the allergen statement on your tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is Ghost protein more expensive than Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Ghost sells smaller tubs at a higher price per serving: $54.99 for 26 servings, or about $2.12 per 25 g, against $79.99 for 58 servings of Gold Standard at Target, about $1.44. Ghost's licensed flavors and added enzymes are part of what buyers pay for. In small tubs the gap narrows, and ON's 1.98 lb tub can cost more.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

